<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fort Orange</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1624</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fort Orange was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fur-trading post built in 1624 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a>, at what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albany-new-york">Albany, New York</a>. It was the main point of trade between New Netherland and the Mohawk and other Iroquois nations, who exchanged beaver pelts for firearms and goods. After the English conquest of 1664 its settlement was renamed Albany.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fort-orange.jpg" alt="Fort Orange" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Orange_in_c1630_Rensselaerswyck_Map_Detail_(now_Albany_NY).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
